It's easier to keep I and YOU than forever distinguish between I as speaker
and YOU as listener. Besides, none of "speaker", "listener", "speak",
"listener", or "-er" (meaning 'one who does X') is part of NSM.

And if the other person is currently speaking, then he is not I.

"I" can't be defined by simpler words. It, along with "YOU", "THIS", "HERE"=
,
"NOW" are the basis of deixis, which I don't think you can (or should)
eliminate.

Anyway, these words have been discussed in Wierzbicka's books, along with
why they are primitive.

stevo

